"<p>Nitrobase's <strong>Cloud Sync</strong> engine supports one-off <strong>import</strong> (cloud → self-hosted) and <strong>export</strong> (self-hosted → empty cloud project) for both <strong>Supabase Cloud</strong> and <strong>Appwrite Cloud</strong>.</p>\n<h2>Supabase Cloud Sync</h2>\n<h3>1. Configure Root CA Certificates (TLS <code>verify-full</code>)</h3>\n<p>Nitrobase connects to Supabase's session pooler using <code>sslmode=verify-full</code>. Place Supabase's root CA certificates (<code>prod-ca-2021.crt</code> and <code>prod-ca-2025.crt</code> from <a href=\"https://github.com/supabase/cli\"><code>supabase/cli</code></a>) into <code>certs/supabase-root-ca.pem</code>, point <code>CLOUD_SYNC_SUPABASE_CA_FILE</code> at your CA bundle, or paste an extra PEM certificate in the connection form.</p>\n<h3>2. Add a Cloud Connection</h3>\n<p>Go to <strong>Settings → Cloud connections → Add connection</strong> and select <strong>Supabase</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Project reference:</strong> The 20-letter reference from <code>https://&#x3C;reference>.supabase.co</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Personal access token:</strong> From Supabase Account → Access tokens (reads project metadata, pooler settings, and edge functions via the Management API).</li>\n<li><strong>Database password:</strong> From Project Settings → Database.</li>\n<li><strong>Storage S3 access key ID &#x26; secret (optional):</strong> From Project Settings → Storage → S3 access keys (required to copy storage files via <code>rclone</code>).</li>\n<li><strong>Pooler host (optional):</strong> Auto-detected from the project's session pooler settings when left empty.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Click <strong>Test connection</strong> to verify Management API, Database (pooler), and Storage (S3) access.</p>\n<h3>3. Import or Export</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Import:</strong> Go to <strong>Apps → New App → Import from Supabase cloud</strong> (<code>app.sync</code> permission). Nitrobase runs a pre-flight check (counting tables, rows, buckets, functions, Postgres version compatibility, and ensuring free disk ≥ <code>2.5×</code> total size), deploys a new self-hosted Supabase app, and copies:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Database</strong> (using a major-matched Postgres client image with the CVE-2025-8714 <code>\\restrict</code> fix)</li>\n<li><strong>Storage files</strong> (parallel <code>rclone</code> transfers)</li>\n<li><strong>Edge functions</strong></li>\n<li><strong>pg_cron jobs:</strong> Recreated <code>paused</code> by default (<code>CLOUD_SYNC_CRON_JOBS=paused</code>) so old URLs aren't called accidentally, or choose <code>active</code> / <code>skip</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Export:</strong> From an existing self-hosted Supabase app's <strong>Cloud sync</strong> tab, export database, storage files, and edge functions into an empty hosted Supabase project (with an option to stop writers during the copy for an exact snapshot).</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Appwrite Cloud Sync</h2>\n<p>Under <strong>Settings → Cloud connections</strong>, you can also connect an <strong>Appwrite Cloud</strong> project (<code>https://&#x3C;region>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1</code>, Project ID, and a scoped temporary API key) to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Import from Appwrite Cloud:</strong> Creates a self-hosted Appwrite app with the <strong>same Project ID</strong> (so client apps only change the endpoint) and migrates <strong>users and teams</strong>, <strong>databases</strong>, <strong>storage files</strong>, <strong>function settings &#x26; variables</strong>, and <strong>platforms</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Export to Appwrite Cloud:</strong> Exports those same resources into an empty Appwrite Cloud project (requires <code>migrations.read</code> and <code>migrations.write</code> scopes on the cloud API key).</li>\n</ul>"